I wasn't able to change anything. The permission prompts for writing the three files weren't approved, and neither was running the tests, so nothing on disk has changed.

The fix I'd make: `src/services/user-service.js` still uses the old callback style for database calls (`query`/`update`). It should use the existing promise-based `UserRepository` instead:

**`src/services/user-service.js`**
```js
import { UserRepository } from '../repositories/user-repository.js';

const users = new UserRepository();

export function listActiveUsers() {
  return users.findActive();
}

export function findUser(id) {
  return users.findById(id);
}

export function softDeleteUser(id, when) {
  return users.softDelete(id, when);
}
```

Changing these functions from callbacks to promises means two other places that use them have to change too:

- **`tests/user-service.test.js`**: both tests become `async` and `await` the functions instead of passing callbacks.
- **`src/api/routes.js`**: only the `/users` and `/users/:id` routes change, so they wait for the result before replying:
  ```js
  handler: (req, cb) => listActiveUsers().then((users) => cb(null, users), cb)
  handler: (req, cb) => findUser(Number(req.params.id)).then((user) => cb(null, user), cb)
  ```

Nothing else calls these functions, so no other files are affected. To check the change I'd run `node --test tests/user-service.test.js` and then `node scripts/check-routes.js`; the second one catches routes that break even when the unit tests pass.

If you approve writes to those three files and those two `node` commands, I'll apply this and run both checks.

